            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Swine primarily symbolize moral and ritual impurity, the profane, apostates returning to sin, and frequently Gentiles or pagans as outsiders to the covenant.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Swine symbolize hypocritical religious insiders—a people who display outward covenant signs without inward reality, specifically identified as the 'Synagogue of Satan' or false Jews.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Uncleanness, the profane, apostates, and outsiders/Gentiles.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Hypocritical covenant members (those who say they are Jews and are not).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes profane outsiders, pagans, and general impurity, restricting the symbol's referent strictly to hypocrites claiming covenant status.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The primary consensus views swine as the archetype of the unholy outsider or the completely profane person; the book identifies swine as the archetype of the hypocritical insider who has the outward form of religion but lacks the inward work.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"While one consensus alternative notes 'hypocrisy' based on the pig's cloven hoof, the consensus primarily views swine as representing gross defilement, the unregenerate, or Gentiles. The book elevates the hoof/cud distinction to the primary meaning, using it to typologically connect swine with false Jews and the 'Synagogue of Satan,' replacing the consensus referent of the profane outsider.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's interpretation relies on allegorizing the biological criteria of the Levitical law (the hoof and the cud) and cross-referencing passages about false Jews (Romans 2, Revelation 2-3) that never mention swine, thereby ignoring the simpler, dominant biblical use of swine as a figure for gross defilement and abomination.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
